AACBridge
Predictive Amortized KV-Caching for Low-Latency Edge LLMs
in Augmentative and Alternative Communication
B.Tech Capstone Project Plan  •  2025–26  •  AI / Healthcare / Embedded Systems
Team: Arjit  •  Medha  •  Heer


This document is a shared planning reference. Everything here is a suggestion — please update, disagree, and edit freely. It’s meant to help coordinate across locations during summer, not to assign blame or rigid ownership. All decisions are collective.


1.  Project Overview
AACBridge is a smartphone-based AAC system for non-verbal patients. It reads facial EMG signals via a BLE wearable and eye-gaze via the front camera, fuses them into an intent, and uses a quantized on-device LLM to generate a natural spoken response in under 500ms.
The primary engineering contribution is KV Cache Priming — precomputing the patient’s personal context (profile, caregiver, time of day, conversation history) as a key-value cache in the background, so the LLM only processes new intent tokens on each call. This eliminates the prefill latency bottleneck on mobile hardware.

Target outcomes:
Working Android prototype: EMG + gaze → speech, TTFT < 500ms end-to-end
Latency ablation: KV-primed vs baseline RAG, across INT4 / INT8 / FP16, N ∈ {50,100,200,500} tokens
Cross-modal fusion ablation: fusion vs EMG-only vs gaze-only F1
Paper submitted to IEEE Access by mid-July 2026
Provisional patent covering KV Cache Priming pipeline + cache invalidation logic


