5.  Critical Architectural Decisions
5.1  EMG Pipeline Fallback  —  Mock Intent Generator
The core paper contribution is KV Cache Priming, not EMG classification. If Medha’s TFLite model is not exportable by June 8, benchmarking must not block.
Arjit implements MockIntentGenerator by end of Phase 1 (May 21): rule-based intent payloads simulating real classifier output
All KV cache priming, latency benchmarking, and drift detection run against the mock generator independently
Real EMG model is swapped in when ready. Results re-run if time permits.
💡 This decouples the two contributions. Reviewers evaluate KV caching on its own merits.

5.2  KV Cache Memory Management
KV cache tensors are large. Unmanaged, they exceed the ~2GB RAM Android allocates to background services.
Context length capped at N=500 tokens per state
Caches quantized to INT4 before flash serialization
Only top-3 states by S(cᵢ) held in active RAM at any time
LRU eviction: least-recently-used cache evicted to flash when new state must load
Memory budget documented with measured figures in paper Implementation section

5.3  Semantic Drift Detector  —  Overhead Budget
Preempt reviewer concern about running an embedding model continuously on-device.
MiniLM-L6-v2 INT8 (~22MB) runs async on Android efficiency cores, post-generation only
Triggered every N=3 turns (~1–2 second frequency)
Target: <5% CPU utilization, measured and reported in ablation study
Does not compete with llama.cpp inference — scheduled on efficiency cores explicitly
k=3 justified empirically: Medha’s k-ablation F1 table (due June 7) provides the evidence

5.4  Cold Start Handling
GeofencingApi only fires on boundary crossing. A reboot inside an existing geofence never triggers the daemon.
BOOT_COMPLETED receiver in AndroidManifest (Heer implements) — daemon wakes before user opens app
Application.onCreate() checks for null llama.cpp RAM pointer (Arjit’s backend, Heer calls it)
If null: active sweep — LocationManager.getLastKnownLocation() + clock + 3s BLE scan
Scores all 50 states, loads top-3 KV caches before user interacts
Two-tier fallback if cold start still running when user triggers intent:
Tier 1: SQLite generic response via Android TTS — <50ms latency
Tier 2: async llama.cpp prefill completes in background, upgrades from next intent onward

5.5  Fusion Model Fallback
Cross-attention fusion carries deployment risk on Android CPU under tight latency constraints.
Primary: cross-attention fusion (Heer’s model) — EMG embedding + gaze vector → unified intent embedding
Fallback: concatenation-based late fusion — simpler, lower latency, still publishable
Decision point June 7: if cross-attention F1 does not exceed late fusion by >3%, switch to fallback and report both in ablation


5.6  Contextual Bandit  —  Adaptive Response Selection
Rather than generating a fixed response per intent, the system adapts phrasing style over time using a Contextual Multi-Armed Bandit (CMAB).
Action space: S = {concise, polite, urgent} — prompt modifiers, not separate LLM calls. Single inference pass preserved.
Update rule: wᵢ ← (1-α)·wᵢ + α·rₜ (EMA, α ∈ [0.1, 0.3]). Weights naturally bounded to [-2, 1] by reward range.
Recent interactions weighted exponentially higher than old ones — correct for AAC where preferences drift with context.
Reward signal (implicit, logged async, never in critical path): +1 accepted, -1 repeated, -2 caregiver override, 0 neutral.
Selection: epsilon-greedy — argmax(wᵢ) with prob (1-ε), random with prob ε.
Persistence: bandit_weights SQLite table (intent, state_id, style) → (weight REAL, count INTEGER). In-memory map at runtime, async flush to DB.
New state initialization: wᵢ=0 ∀i → uniform exploration → EMA adapts quickly.
Module: ResponseSelector.kt (Arjit). Reward triggers defined and fired by Heer from MainActivity.
Cuttable: Yes — if implementation slips, remove without affecting core KV caching contribution.
6.  Key Risks & Suggested Mitigations
Risk
Owner
Suggested Mitigation
[CRITICAL] Medha’s TFLite export not ready by June 8 — blocks pipeline integration
Medha
Arjit’s MockIntentGenerator is the fallback. Test TFLite export by June 1 as dry run. Flag issues immediately.
[CRITICAL] KV cache size exceeds 2GB Android RAM budget
Arjit
N=500 token cap, INT4 quantization, top-3 only in RAM, LRU eviction. Measure and document in paper.
Drift detector CPU overhead flagged by reviewer
Arjit
Measure <5% CPU on efficiency cores. Report profiling numbers in ablation. Run async, not in inference critical path.
Cross-attention fusion too slow on Android CPU
Heer
Fallback to concatenation late fusion if F1 gap <3% at June 7 decision point. Report both in ablation.
Cold start: RAM wiped on reboot, geofence never fires
Heer + Arjit
BOOT_COMPLETED receiver (Heer) + active sweep (Arjit backend). Two-tier fallback ensures no conversation halt.
Snapdragon device unavailable for benchmarking
Arjit
Book device now. If unavailable: run benchmarks on PC first, rerun on device before submission.
DailyDialog annotation too slow
Medha
Annotate 100 conversations minimum. Sufficient for F1 calculation. Don’t wait for perfect coverage.
Placement season overlap (July 8+)


All
All benchmarking done by June 25. Writing can overlap with placements. Engineering cannot.
Bandit weights reset on reboot if async flush not implemented correctly
Arjit
In-memory map must flush to SQLite before process kill. Use Android onTrimMemory() callback to trigger flush on low-memory warning



7.  Tech Stack Reference
Arjit — Backend / LLM
llama.cpp (ARM64, NDK build) — KV cache APIs: llama_state_save_seq, llama_state_load_seq
JNI (Java Native Interface) — C++ ↔ Kotlin bridge
ONNX Runtime Android — MiniLM-L6-v2 INT8 for drift detection
Model: Phi-3-Mini or Qwen2.5-1.5B, Q4_K_M quantization (~1.1–2.2GB)
Room (SQLite) — 50 context states + fallback response store
Weights & Biases — experiment tracking for all benchmark runs

Medha — Input / ML
Python + SciPy / NumPy — EMG signal filtering and windowing
PyTorch — CNN-LSTM intent classifier
TFLite / ONNX — model export for Android deployment
scikit-learn — baseline classifiers and evaluation metrics
NinaPro DB5 — public EMG dataset
DailyDialog — topic-shift annotation corpus for k-ablation

Heer — App / UI + Fusion
Kotlin + Android SDK — app layer, ContextDaemon, UI
Android APIs: GeofencingApi, BluetoothLeScanner + PendingIntent, WorkManager, AlarmManager, LocationManager
MediaPipe Face Mesh — gaze fixation detection from front camera
PyTorch — cross-attention fusion model training
ONNX — fusion model export for Android

Shared / Paper
Overleaf (LaTeX) — IEEE Access two-column template
Zotero — reference management
GitHub — version control, mandatory (use it)
iThenticate — plagiarism check before submission

Target Journal
PRIMARY: IEEE Access — open access, Scopus-indexed, ~4–6 week review. Applied systems + empirical benchmarking scope. Submit mid-July → “Under review at IEEE Access” on resume during placement season.
Fallback: Computers in Biology and Medicine (Elsevier, Scopus Q1).
NOT targeting AAAI — requires theoretical proofs, clinical user studies, SOTA comparisons. Not feasible on this timeline with simulated data.


